\f0\fs24 \cf2 <$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 In the pre-dawn darkness of a long sunny Wednesday on the twenty-third of a December, the Ulyssian Defence Force deployed a forward contingent of ordnance and troops to knock on Eloise Shakti\'92s Sublime Point front door.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 They passed through the village as best they could on the narrow roads, past housing and fairways. On the final approach to the Shakti compound, the road was even narrower than it appeared on the maps. Parts of the convoy pulled over where they found space amongst the scrub. Only metres to the left, the bush fell off the plateau into the valley. The road swept gently to the left and straightened and rose gradually. Some of the convoy peeled off to the right on a bush track that led to the compound perimeter farther west.\
Ahead, the road widened. Either side, the fencing appeared gradually in the convoy headlights\'97tall enough to discourage all but determined climbers. One moment it appeared to be made of triangular panels of \cf0 \outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 bush-smoked material \cf2 \outl0\strokewidth0 set at small angles to one another. The next moment it appeared constructed of dark tendrils that caught and reflected the light. Then the triangles again, some of which seemed to transition between opaque and transparent without ever allowing the eye to accurately see beyond.\
The soldiers in the lead vehicle felt their eyes ache as they tried to focus on the fence and their scalps tighten. Blinking didn\'92t help; only looking away seemed to dispel the disorientation and the sense of oncoming headache. The driver slowed the truck and stopped just beyond where the road widened. She knew not to drive beyond the fencing. Though the gates weren\'92t up, the convoy had been warned it would be unsafe to pass through, especially if something like a fine mist seemed to hang across the road beyond the fencing. The briefing explained the illusion of mist was generated by an energetic membrane stretched across the road at the fence line. The membrane itself was not dangerous, but signified the particle weapons deployed beneath the road were active.\
Three soldiers walked past the lead truck to inspect the membrane. As they approached, they could not gauge its exact placement, although they knew it ran straight and flat between the sides of the fence. Close up, it defied description\'97the briefing\'92s 
\f2\i smoke-dark semi-transparent distortion
\f1\i0  would do well enough. Beyond it, nothing seemed to hold its depth. Reality seemed to have cracked in places; road edges failed to meet; segments of tree trunks were displaced sideways by centimetres, as if viewed in a broken mirror. Fine triangular patterns formed and dissolved across the plane of the membrane, shifting like ice breaking up on a frozen lake.\
One soldier walked forward; when she came within a metre, narrow violet bars appeared in the membrane, directly in front, extending a bar or two beyond her own width and a little taller than her height. She took two steps left. Some bars faded while   others glowed up. The soldier felt like she was being followed.\
Another soldier walked to the scrub and picked up a light leafy branch, slightly longer than his arm, that had fallen from a nearby gum tree. He approached the membrane; violet bars appeared as if blocking his path. He slowly poked the leafy end of the branch forward and felt resistance as it touched the membrane. He eased more weight into it, until the resistance yielded and leaves passed into the plane of the membrane as if it was nothing more than smoke. The violet bars flashed and several leaves dropped from the branch at the soldier\'92s feet.\
The soldier pulled the branch out. Where a bar crossed the spray of foliage, it had cut straight through. Leaves hung in halves from their stems. Foliage that had not touched the bars remained undamaged. He pushed the branch back in, then moved it sideways through the violet bars. They flashed. Short lengths of twig fell onto the road. A strip of bark lifted cleanly from the thicker stem. He moved the branch farther sideways. Violet lines lit up to meet the intrusion and pieces of it dropped on both sides of the membrane, leaving him holding a length of wood with most of its leafy end removed as if by guillotine.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 <!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 Major Midler of the UDF made several attempts to set up a command post and was met with local resistance in the form of legal teams, having been summoned from their beds and favourite night clubs to wave restraining orders and declare that camouflage tents, artillery and jack boots were not in keeping with village character. Eventually, Midler signed in to Airborne Bed\'92n\'92Banquet app, and booked a backyard pool house by the golf course, although accepting the terms and conditions irked him, especially the clause forbidding cigar-smoking while swimming in the pool.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 Communications gear was still being loaded in when a contingent of residents marched on the pool house\'97some still in night gowns, others accompanied by their poodles and wolfhounds\'97and demanded to see every soldier\'92s working with children check. Another contingent arrived and demanded to know the meaning of the incursion, and then ruled Major Midler\'92s reply as out of order for discussing military matters. Others demanded to know if the major worked for a private army or the communist government one. Others threatened to commission an acoustic analysis and said that, while they didn\'92t mind the sound of gunshot, they would not abide idling trucks in their streets. Others threatened that hindrance of council vehicles\'92 access to garbage bins would be met with the kind of litigation that would send Major Midler\'92s ancestors broke. Wolfhounds and 
\f2\i shih tzus
\f1\i0  barked happily about that. One resident showed up with a bleary-eyed lawyer, and demanded to see the prospectus and an application form to invest. A patrician resident arrived in slippers and a cat sleeping in her hair. While both the old woman and the indeterminately aged cat looked unhappy, neither spoke a word.\
The sun hadn\'92t come up yet. At least 
\f2\i it
\f1\i0  wasn\'92t complaining.\
Major Midler ordered troops to hold positions. Inevitably, some small skirmishes occurred between a few ill-disciplined UDF deployments and Shakti technology. None of the UDF got a shot off, though Shakti defences sizzled ground cover, brought down eucalyptus branches and melted neat holes through bush rock. A surveillance camera in one soldier\'92s pack turned to hot slag, delivering no more than a warm tickle before the soldier had slung the pack to the ground.\
Major Midler ordered reconnaissance drones.\
One crossed a boundary only Shakti defences appeared able to see and became fireworks. A second moved low through the trees and exploded, startling a cuddle of koalas in the throes of their raucous reproduction rights. Another drone climbed, circled and survived long enough to transmit several seconds of excellent landscape footage,  suitable for a tourism campaign, before fragments of it went off to explore different parts of the ground cover.\
In the command centre pool house, Major Midler\'92s officers went back to their maps on tablets as big as atlases, and reported they had \'93taken\'94 the fifty-odd hectares of bush area between the golf course and the Shakti compound. The major thought rather, they had been allowed to take it.\
Stripped of acronyms and optimism, Major Midler framed his own sit-rep. He could probably take Sublime Point, but he wasn\'92t going to enjoy it. When he queried the higher brass about where his orders originated, he was told gruffly to mind his own business and to ignore any rumours that CEO Dunhilton might have personally ordered it, certainly not from his phone, and certainly not while apparently\'97judging by the certainly not acoustics\'97negotiating a certainly not especially recalcitrant bowel movement.\
Such were the winks and nods among professional military personnel. No officer up and down the line would be at ease with being deployed among their own civilian population.\
As the sun came up, he lit his first cigar and flicked ashes into the pool.\
Not long after, UDF hover-copts came from the south-east, defying gravity to stay in the air, as they took up surveillance positions east, south and west of the compound over the Jamison Valley.
